# COMPOSITE-TOKEN-HIJACK (docs/DESIGN.md Appendix A; docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 6;
# rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.2).  Deferred at §13 step 1 as findings F5/F20: seeding
# it before its contributors existed was a guaranteed E051 lint failure.  All
# three now ship - CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_LONG_EXPIRY-01 (§8.5, CLOUD-23),
# DAST-LEAK-JS_CONFIG-01 (§7.1, DAST-10) and DAST-GQL-INTROSPECTION-01 (§7.4,
# DAST-27) - so the seed lands here rather than staying a fixture-only proof.
#
# The chain is a property of one deployed front end: a long-lived AppSync API
# key (a static, unrotated bearer credential), that same class of key served in
# the JavaScript bundle a browser downloaded (so the credential is not merely
# long-lived but already exposed to anyone who fetched the page), and GraphQL
# introspection left enabled (so whoever now holds the key also holds a
# complete map of every type, field and mutation it can reach). Each
# contributor alone is a medium; together they are the critical finding a
# reader acts on first. All three are REQUIRED - this is one chain, not a set
# of interchangeable alternatives - so every contributor is `requires`, and
# `correlate-on: target` is the only correlation key all three can supply
# (DAST via `loc_target`; the cloud contributor via §9.2.2's endpoint-host
# attribution against config/scope.conf).

id: COMPOSITE-TOKEN-HIJACK
kind: derived
title: A long-lived AppSync API key is exposed in served JavaScript, and its schema is fully queryable via introspection
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-522
owasp: A07:2021
requires: CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_LONG_EXPIRY-01
requires: DAST-LEAK-JS_CONFIG-01
requires: DAST-GQL-INTROSPECTION-01
correlate-on: target
tags: derived
remediation: Rotate the AppSync API key immediately - it is not merely
  long-lived, it has already been observed served to every visitor of the
  bundle, so treat it as compromised rather than merely stale. Then close all
  three links so the chain cannot re-form: move the API off API_KEY
  authentication onto AWS_IAM or Amazon Cognito user pools so a captured
  credential alone is no longer sufficient; stop shipping the key to the
  browser and have it held server-side instead; and disable introspection on
  the production API so a future leaked key does not also hand out a complete
  map of what it can reach. Any one of these three closes the composite, but
  only rotating the key addresses the credential already exposed.
references: CWE-522
references: CWE-613
references: CWE-540
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A07_2021-Identification_and_Authentication_Failures/
